  "final": "I've secured both functions. `get_document` and `list_documents` now only return documents that match the given tenant and owner and are not deleted. The existing check and a new one I added both pass.\n\n**Changes**\n- **`src/repository.py`**:\n  - `find(connection, tenant, owner, document_id)` now filters on `id=? AND tenant=? AND owner=? AND deleted_at IS NULL`.\n  - `listing(connection, tenant, owner)` now filters on `tenant=? AND owner=? AND deleted_at IS NULL ORDER BY id`. Before, it filtered by owner only, so it could return other tenants' and deleted documents.\n  - Every value is passed as a bound `?` parameter, never pasted into the SQL text. Results still contain only `id`, `title` and `body`, with no deletion data.\n- **`src/service.py`**: now passes `tenant` and `owner` through to the repository. The function signatures are unchanged.\n- An unavailable ID always comes back as plain `None`. The same single query handles every case, so the result doesn't reveal whether a record belongs to another tenant or owner, was deleted, or doesn't exist.\n\n**Checks**\n- `python3 checks/public.py` passes.\n- New `checks/access.py` passes. It confirms that:\n  - `get_document` returns `None` for documents from another tenant, from another owner, deleted, or missing.\n  - Lists are ordered by id and leave out deleted and other users' documents.\n  - Tenant and owner names containing quotes (like `q'x` and `o'; DROP TABLE documents;--`) find the right record.\n  - Injection-style inputs like `t' OR '1'='1` return nothing, and the table is unchanged afterwards.",
